    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "You can now put a person on hold and take them off it again, and `bin/e2e` passes all 4 tests in the spec.\n\n**How it behaves:**\n- Each person's row has a **Hold / Release** button (`person-hold-toggle`) and shows hold as `yes`/`no` (`person-hold`). If the action fails, the row's existing `person-error` message appears.\n- Holding writes `PERSON_HELD \u2026 name=<name>` and releasing writes `PERSON_RELEASED \u2026 name=<name>`. If the totals move as a result, the usual `ROLLUP` and `TOTALS` lines follow.\n- While someone is held, their open claims are left out of their own claimed and due-total, the sheet's totals, and a period's total. That covers both the live total on screen and the total fixed when the period is closed. Their settled claims still count.\n\n**What changed:**\n- **Stored data:** migration `V17__person_held.sql` adds a `held` column to people.\n- **Server:**\n  - `Person` now has a `held` flag and a new endpoint, `POST /api/sheets/{id}/people/{personId}/hold`, switches it on or off.\n  - The rule for which claims count now lives in one place, `RollupService.counts(person, claim)`. The sheet totals, period closing and statements all use it.\n- **Front end:** `ClaimsService.live()` applies the same rule on screen, and the people and period totals use it.\n\n**One decision for you:** I applied the hold to statements too. A statement taken while someone is held leaves out their open claims and doesn't lock in those claims' due amounts. The request didn't mention statements and the tests don't cover this case. If you'd rather statements ignore the hold, it's a one-line change in `ClaimService.statement`.",
